course: dictionary
lesson: 4867
title: Icicle, a hanging piece of ice
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An icicle is a long, pointed piece of ice that hangs down. You can work out its shape from the word: it is thin and sharp at the end, and it points to the ground.
explain: Icicles form when water drips and freezes in cold air. Context helps here: «icicles hanging from the roof» tells you where you are likely to see them, and winter tells you why.
explain: «Never stand under icicles or knock them down, because they are heavy and sharp.» The words heavy and sharp are clues that an icicle can hurt you. When you read about danger, look for the clue words.
fact: An icicle is a long, pointed piece of ice that hangs down.
fact: Icicles form when dripping water freezes in cold air.
fact: «Icicles» can fall from roofs, so never stand under them.
fact: Never knock down an icicle, because it is heavy and sharp.
fact: Do not lick metal or eat icicles from roofs.
exercise: What is an icicle? | *A long, pointed piece of ice that hangs down | A warm drip of water on a window | A round snowball rolled along the ground
exercise: «Never stand under icicles or knock them down, because they are heavy and sharp.» Why should you not stand under them? | Because they are warm and soft | Because they make a nice shade | *Because they can fall and hurt you
exercise: Icicles form when water drips and what happens to it? | *It freezes in cold air | It turns into steam | It disappears into the ground
test: «Snow piles can collapse, and icicles can fall from roofs.» Where can icicles fall from? | *The roof | The grass | The pond | The playground slide
test: Why does an icicle form in winter? | Ice melts into the air | The sun makes ice hot | Snow turns into sand | *Dripping water freezes in cold air
exam: Which action is safe near icicles? | Knock them down with a stick | Walk under them to look up | Lick the icicles hanging from the roof | *Stay well away from them and never lick them
